<section xml:id="sec_rc-charge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Charging a Capacitor</title>
	
					<activity>
						<title>Predictions</title>
						<introduction>
							<p>
								
							</p>
							<p>The circuit below contains a battery, a resistor, a switch, and a capacitor. The capacitor is initially uncharged and the voltage of the battery is <m>V</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="RC1">
			  <title>Light Bulb and Capacitor Circuit Diagram</title>
			<caption>A circuit diagram with both a resistor and a capacitor.</caption>
			<image source="RC-circuit.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a battery, open switch, resistor, and capacitor.</description></image>
		</figure>
						</introduction>
						<task>
							<statement>
								<p>Just after the switch is closed: what is the absolute value of the voltage across the capacitor?  Explain your reasoning.</p>
							</statement>
						</task>
						<task>
							<statement>
								<p>
									A long time after the switch is closed: what is the absolute value of the voltage across the capacitor?  Explain your reasoning.
								</p>
							</statement>
						</task>
						<task>
							<statement>
								<p>
									Sketch a graph predicting the <em>current</em> through the circuit as a function of time, starting at <m>t = 0</m>.  Comment on the value of the current in the limit as <m>t \rightarrow \infty</m>.
								</p>
							</statement>
						</task>
						<task>
							<statement>
								<p>
									How does this circuit seem different from when the capacitor discharged instead?  How is it similar?
								</p>
							</statement>
						</task>
						<task>
							<statement>
								<p>
									Try building this circuit in the <q>RLC</q> tab of the <url href="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/circuit-construction-kit-ac/latest/circuit-construction-kit-ac_all.html">Circuits PhET</url> simulation to check your predictions.  You can use the current chart feature on the left to view a graph of the current.
								</p>
							</statement>
						</task>
					</activity>

						<corollary xml:id="vid-charging-capacitor">
	<title>Charging Capacitor</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="EuU7Me6dvzg"><description>How to figure out the charge on a charging capacitor.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>
					
	<subsubsection><title>Derivation: Charging Capacitor</title></subsubsection>


	<p>
		As with the discharging capacitor, you can use the <xref ref="loop-rule"/> to relate the voltages across the different circuit elements: 
		<me>V_{bat} + V_C + V_R = 0</me>
		<me>V - I(t)R - \frac{Q(t)}{C} = 0</me>
		You can now leverage that the current through the resistor <m>I(t)</m> is the rate of change at which charge <m>Q(t)</m> is <em>increasing</em> on the positive plate, giving <m>I(t) = \frac{dQ(t)}{dt}</m>
		<me>V - \frac{Q(t)}{C} = R\frac{dQ(t)}{dt}</me>
		<me>\frac{dQ(t)}{dt} = \frac{V}{R} - \frac{Q(t)}{RC}</me>
		You can solve this differential equation to find the charge on the capacitor as a function of time:
		<me>Q(t) = CV \left(1 - e^{-t/RC}\right)</me>
	</p>

	<activity>
		<title>Charging and Discharging</title>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			What similarities do you see between the <alert>charging capacitor</alert> and the <alert>discharging capacitor</alert>?  What differences do you see?
		</p>
	</activity>
	


